{"qid":"Q847420","names":["Mamluk Dynasty"],"title":"Mamluk Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Mamluk dynasty of Delhi (also called the Slave dynasty or Ghulam dynasty) was the first ruling house of the Delhi Sultanate, founded in 1206 when Qutb ud-Din Aibak, a Turkic slave-general of Muhammad of Ghor, declared independence following his master's assassination. The dynasty's most notable ruler, Iltutmish (r. 1211–1236), consolidated the sultanate, repelled the western advance of the Mongols, and erected the Qutb Minar complex in Delhi. Razia Sultana (r. 1236–1240) briefly reigned as the subcontinent's first female Muslim sovereign. The line ended in 1290 when the last sultan, Muiz ud-Din Qaiqabad, was overthrown by Jalal ud-Din Firuz Khalji, inaugurating the Khalji dynasty; the dataset's to-date of 1294 reflects attested Cliopatria geometry through that year.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent of the early Delhi Sultanate is reconstructed from chronicles such as the Tabaqat-i-Nasiri; boundaries are approximate, especially for frontier zones toward Rajputana and the Gangetic plain.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Mamluk dynasty (Delhi)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamluk_dynasty_(Delhi)"},{"label":"Britannica: Slave dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Slave-dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Iltutmish","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Iltutmish"}],"britannica":"topic/Slave-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Mamluk dynasty (Delhi)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Shajar al-Durr","nativeName":"شجر الدر","roman":"Shajarat al-Durr","start":125